{"id":"positions-spending-vs-filled","title":"Spending up, filled positions down","question":"In which departments did spending rise between fiscal 2019 and fiscal 2026 while the number of filled positions fell?","finding":"Citywide, filled positions rose 3.7% between fiscal 2019 and fiscal 2026 (34,936 to 36,211) while spending rose 39%; the typical job class’s top pay step rose 31% over the same years. In 10 departments positions fell while spending rose, and in 8 of those spending rose by a quarter or more: Sheriff (positions -13%, spending +51%); War Memorial (positions -9%, spending +51%); Juvenile Probation (positions -20%, spending +39%); Police (positions -15%, spending +40%); Adult Probation (positions -10%, spending +39%).","how":"Filled positions are the Controller’s count of actual full-time-equivalent staff paid from permanent salaries, by department and fiscal year. Spending is every spending line for the department in the Controller’s Spending and Revenue table, and salaries are its \"Salaries\" lines. Fiscal 2019 is compared with fiscal 2026, the last complete year. Departments with fewer than 50 positions in 2019 are left out. Rows where positions fell and spending rose come first.","limits":"Pay rose for everyone over these years, so spending rises even when headcount is flat; the salaries column shows how much of the rise is pay. Unfilled posts are often covered by overtime, and a department can move work to contractors or grants, take on debt service or capital projects, or pass money through to other bodies. The positions count leaves out temporary staff. This does not show that any work was contracted out to avoid civil-service rules; the files on disk do not mark exempt positions or contracting-out approvals. The payroll file shows a full-time year of 2,160 paid hours in fiscal 2026 against 2,080 in other years (27 fortnights, not 26), so up to 3.8 points of any rise in salaries to fiscal 2026 may be the calendar and not pay or staffing.","tie